Case (ii): If $(b, j) \ne (\tau(b'), j')$ and either  $b \in {\mathcal E}^+$, $b' \in {\mathcal E}^-$ or  $b \in {\mathcal E}^-$, $b' \in {\mathcal E}^+$, we define
\[
Z_{(b, j)}^{(b', j')}(v_0^{j''}(b''))= \begin{cases} v_0^{j'}(b'), &\text{ if $(b'', j'') = (b, j)$;}\\ v_0^j(\tau(b)), &\text{ if $(b'', j'') = (\tau(b'), j')$;} \\  0, &\text{ otherwise.} \end{cases}
\]

This means
\[
\xi_{(b_1, j_1)}^{(b_2,  j_2)} = \begin{cases} 1, &\text{ if $(b_1, j_1) = ( b, j)$ and $(b_2, j_2) = (\tau(b'), j')$;}\\ 1, &\text{ if $(b_1, j_1) = ( \tau(b'), j')$ and $(b_2, j_2) = (b, j)$;}\\ 0, &\text{otherwise.} \end{cases}
\]

We get easily that  
\[
\langle Z_{(b, j)}^{(b', j')}(v), v' \rangle_{\mathbf c} + \langle v, Z_{(b, j)}^{(b', j')}(v')\rangle_{\mathbf c} = 0 
\]
 for all $v, v' \in V_0$ from the  equations~(\ref{CoordinateEquationSymplecticOdd}).  Note that  the pair $\tau(b'), \tau(b)$ falls also in the  case (ii) and it is easy to see that $Z_{(\tau(b'), j')}^{(\tau(b), j)} =  Z_{(b, j)}^{(b', j')}$ by evaluating both sides on the basis vectors $v_0^{j''}(b'')$.

