Observe that $\hat{p}$ is increasing with the sample size.  This is to be expected, since the
optimal value of $p$ should be infinite for the uniform distribution.  
Let $F_n(x)$ denote the distribution function of $\sqrt{nh}(\widehat{a} - a)$.  Then
\begin{small}
	\begin{equation*}
		F_n(c_0, c_1) = P(\sqrt{nh} (\widehat{a_j} - a_j) \leq c_j; j=0,1) = P(\widehat{a_j} \leq a_j + c_j/\sqrt{nh}) = P(\ell'(a_j + c_j/\sqrt{nh}) > 0). (\star\star)
	\end{equation*}	
\end{small}
Substituting ($\star$) for ($\star\star$), where vector $a$ replaced by $ a+ c/\sqrt{nh}$, gives
\begin{small}
	\begin{eqnarray} 
		F_n(c_0, c_1) = P\left(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-x)^j |y_i -(a_j + c_j/\sqrt{nh}(x_i-x)|^{p-1} \mbox{sign}(y_i - a_0 - a_1(x_i-x)) K_h(x_i - x) \right) \ge 0
		\nonumber  \\
		= P\left(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-x)^j|\varepsilon_i - c_j/\sqrt{nh}(x_i-x)+r(x_i,x)|^{p-1} \mbox{sign}(\varepsilon_i - c_j/\sqrt{nh}(x_i-x)) K_h(x_i - x)\right) \ge 0 	\nonumber 
	\end{eqnarray} 

\end{small} 
We make use of Taylor expansion for $c_j/\sqrt{nh}(x_i-x)^j?$ so we have:
\begin{small}
	\begin{eqnarray} 
		P\left(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-x)^j\left[|\epsilon_i|^{p-1} \mbox{sign}(\epsilon_i)- c_j/\sqrt{nh}(x_i-x)^j|\varepsilon_i|^{p-2}(p-1)+r(x_i,x)|\varepsilon_i|^{p-2}(p-1)\right] K_h(x_i - x) \right)\ge 0 \nonumber
	\end{eqnarray} 
\end{small}
multiplying $\times \sqrt{h/n}$, we would have the following,


\begin{small}
	\begin{eqnarray} 
		P(\sqrt{h/n}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-x)^j|\varepsilon_i|^{p-1} \mbox{sign}(\varepsilon_i) K_h(x_i - x)-\nonumber  \\
		\sqrt{h/n}\sum_{i=1}^n c_j/\sqrt{nh}(x_i-x)^j|\varepsilon_i|^{p-2}(p-1) K_h(x_i-x)+\nonumber  \\
		\sqrt{h/n}\sum_{i=1}^nr(x_i,x)|\varepsilon_i|^{p-2}(p-1) K_h(x_i - x) )\ge 0 \nonumber
	\end{eqnarray} 
\end{small}

simplify each term, solve the linear equations for $c_0,c_1$.

