\title{Metric dimension reduction modulus for logarithmic distortion}
\author[D.\ J.\ Altschuler \and K.\ Tikhomirov]{
        Dylan J. Altschuler \and 
        Konstantin Tikhomirov 
        }

\address{Dylan J. Altschuler, Department of Mathematical Sciences, Carnegie Mellon University.}
\address{Konstantin Tikhomirov, Department of Mathematical Sciences, Carnegie Mellon University.}
\begin{document}


\begin{abstract}
Given parameters $n$ and $\alpha$,
the metric dimension reduction modulus $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$ is defined
as the smallest $k$ such that every $n$--point metric space can be embedded into some $k$-dimensional normed space $X$ with bi--Lipschitz distortion at most $\alpha$.
A fundamental task in the theory of metric embeddings
is to obtain sharp asymptotics for $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$
for all choices of $\alpha$ and $n$,
with the range $\alpha=\Theta(\log n)$ bearing special importance.
While advances in the theory lead to the upper bound
$k^{\alpha}_n(\ell_\infty) = O(\log n)$ for $\alpha=\Theta(\log n)$,
obtaining a matching lower bound has remained an open problem.
We prove that $k^{\beta\log n}_n(\ell_\infty) = \Omega(\log n)$
for every constant $\beta>0$,
thereby closing the long--standing gap and resolving a question from the 2018 ICM plenary lecture of Naor. 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle
\section{Introduction}


\subsection{Motivation and main results}
Following the seminal dimension reduction result of Johnson and Lindenstrauss \cite{JLlemma}, it has become a core line of research in metric geometry to characterize metric spaces which admit low--distortion embeddings into low--dimensional normed spaces. 
The next notion helps formalize the problem:

\begin{definition}[Metric dimension reduction modulus; see \cite{naor-snapshot}]
    Let $n$ be a natural number, and let $\alpha\in[1,\infty)$.
Let $X$ be a normed space, possibly infinite dimensional.
Denote by $k^\alpha_n(X)$ the minimum $k \in\mathbb{N}$
such that every $n$ point subset $S\subset X$ embeds with bi-Lipschitz distortion
at most $\alpha$ into some 
$k$--dimensional
linear subspace $F_S$ of $X$ (with respect to the metric induced by $X$). That is, there is a mapping $f:S\to F_S$ satisfying
\[
    \|f(s)-f(s')\|_X\leq \|s-s'\|_X\leq \alpha \,\|f(s)-f(s')\|_X,
\quad \mbox{ for all }s,s'\in S.\\
\]
\end{definition}

The quantity $k^\alpha_n(X)$, particularly with $X = \ell_\infty$, plays a crucial role in the theory of metric embeddings and has been commensurately studied in literature, starting with the seminal paper \cite{Bourgain-Lipschitz} by Bourgain. Since every finite metric space and every finite-dimensional normed space can be isometrically embedded into $\ell_\infty$, it follows that $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$ is
the minimum $k$ such that every $n$--point metric space can be embedded into some $k$-dimensional normed space $X$ with distortion at most $\alpha$. A subset of the works developing bounds on $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$ includes \cite{Bourgain-Lipschitz,JLS87,deReynaPiazza92,LLR95,Matousek-distortion,ABN2011,Naor2017,naor-snapshot}; we refer the reader to the 2018 ICM plenary lecture \cite[Section 1.3]{naor-snapshot} for a comprehensive historical overview.

The focus of this article is on the asymptotics of $k_n^\alpha(\ell_\infty)$ with $\alpha = \Theta(\log n)$. A foundational work of Bourgain \cite{Bourgain-Lipschitz} asserts that every $n$--point metric space can be embedded
in a Hilbert space with a logarithmic distortion. 
Upon combining this result with the Johnson--Lindenstrauss lemma and
Dvoretzky's theorem \cite{Dvoretzky}, one immediately obtains that
for {\it every} infinite--dimensional Banach space $X$
and every sufficiently large constant $\beta>0$,
\begin{equation}\label{modulusupperbound}
k^{\beta\log n}_n(\ell_\infty) \le k^{\beta\log n}_n(X)=O(\log n).    
\end{equation}
The upper bound $k^{\beta\log n}_n(\ell_\infty)=O(\log n)$
in fact holds for every constant $\beta>0$ \cite{Matousek-distortion,ABN2011}.
On the other hand, the strongest
available (up to this writing) lower bound on $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$ 
for the range $\alpha = \Theta(\log n)$ is
$$
k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)=\Omega\Big(\frac{\log n}{\log\log\log n}\Big)
$$
(see formulas (12) and (16) in \cite{naor-snapshot} for further details and other ranges of $\alpha$).
The attractive problem of closing this long--standing gap appears as Question 22 in the ICM plenary lecture
\cite{naor-snapshot}.
\begin{problem}[Naor \cite{naor-snapshot}]
    Determine the asymptotics of $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$ in the regime $\alpha=\Theta(\log n)$.
\end{problem}

We give a complete resolution to this problem:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
Let $\beta>0$ be an arbitrary constant. Then, as $n \to \infty$,
$$
k^{\beta\log n}_n(\ell_\infty)=\Omega(\log n).
$$
\end{theorem}
The lower bound in the theorem matches \eqref{modulusupperbound}
up to implicit constant multiples.
Theorem~\ref{thm:bilipschitz}, proved in the last section of this note, is a more general technical result providing estimates for $k^{\alpha}_n(\ell_\infty)$
in a wider range of $\alpha$ (see Theorem~\ref{thm:bilipschitz}
and remark after it for details). 

The $n$--point metric space producing the lower--bound in \cref{thm:main}, will be provided by (a specifically tailored model of) random graphs.  The metric embeddings of graphs is an important research direction
on its own,
with notable algorithmic applications.
Starting with landmark work \cite{LLR95},
graph embeddings have been actively studied
in both deterministic and randomized settings.
We refer, in particular, to recent survey \cite{Eskenazis2022}
as well as papers \cite{Naor14,MN14,MN15,ADTT2024} for a detailed
overview and further references on the subject.

\subsection{Overview of the proof}\label{sec:overview}
The proof of the main result is based on two key ingredients:
\begin{itemize}
\item First, we employ a specialized model of ultra--sparse, connected random graphs that have slow expansion and, nonetheless, comparable entropy to the uniform random regular model. These will provide the nonembeddable metric spaces.
\item The second ingredient is
a {\it discretization scheme} for mappings of $n$--point spaces into normed spaces. This discretization is adapted from \cite{AT2024}, where it was developed for treating the geometric embeddability of uniform random regular graphs. It was suggested by Naor\footnote{private communication} that
this discretization scheme can be applied to 
the study of the asymptotics of $k^\alpha_n(\ell_\infty)$.
\end{itemize}

Let us describe the first main component in more detail.
While the asymptotic regime of large $\beta$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:main} can be treated using, e.g., uniform 3-regular random graphs, our analysis of 
small values of $\beta$ hinges crucially on the properties of our specially tailored model of random graphs. In particular, a uniform $\Delta$--regular random graph for $\Delta \ge 3$ has too strong of expansion (the volume of vertex neighborhoods grows too fast with increasing radius) for our argument to work when $\beta$ is small. 
On the other hand, the configuration model for graphs with a prescribed degree sequence with the average degree $2+o(1)$, which one may view as a natural remedy to the aforementioned problem, produces disconnected graphs w.h.p, and either conditioning on being connected or studying metric properties of the giant component of such a graph appears to be a very challenging path to go. 

Our approach is instead to generate a graph by constructing a random Hamiltonian $n$--cycle, which ensures connectivity, and then add a partial matching of the appropriate size. Varying the size of the matching allows us fine control over the expansion of the graph. This construction turns out to be highly amenable to analysis, and it also appears to be completely novel specifically in the context of metric embeddings. 

\begin{remark}
   Similar constructions called \textit{Hamiltonian decompositions}, in which the matching is perfect rather than partial, have been considered in the literature for quick, approximate sampling of random 3--regular graphs. We refer the reader to \cite{wormald} for discussion of these models and related results.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Asymptotic notation}
All asymptotics in this paper are with respect to $n$ (the size of the the metric space) diverging.
We write $f=O(g)$, $f=o(g)$
whenever $f$ remains bounded with respect to $g$ (respectively, vanishes) as $n\to \infty$. Further, $f=\Omega(g)$ whenever $g=O(f)$, and $f=\Theta(g)$
if simultaneously $f=O(g)$ and $f=\Omega(g)$.

\bigskip

The article is organized as follows. The construction and analysis of the specialized model of graphs is carried out in \cref{sec:graph}. The discretization is defined in \cref{sec:discretization}. In \cref{sec:bilipschitz}, non-embedding of a typical graph under the specialized model is shown by way of union bounding over (a discretization of) all normed spaces and all embeddings. This yields the technical estimate \cref{thm:bilipschitz}, of which \cref{thm:main} is an immediate corollary.

