\ \\ In this case, the Reeb graph is shown to be a graph whose 1st Betti number is $1$ and which has exactly two vertices where the function $\overline{f}:=\overline{{{f}^{\prime}}_{\tilde{M},a,b,F}}:W_{{{f}^{\prime}}_{\tilde{M},a,b,F}} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ has extrema and exactly two vertices where the function does not have extrema: for the latter vertices the function does not have local extrema. The first two vertices are of degree $2$. The remaining two vertices are of degree $2$ and to each of these vertices exactly two singular points of the function are mapped. In this case, by arguments similar to ones in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:2}, $F$ is of genus $3$. \\

