\begin{comment}

Figure~\ref{fig:most_similar_algorithms} lists the 30 algorithm pairs with the highest mean similarity score. Each row denotes a pair of algorithms, together with their corresponding algorithm group from the MEALPY library. The first two columns contain the similarity scores in two dimensions, while the third column contains the mean of these scores. Finally, the last column contains a boolean indicator of whether the two algorithms belong to the same group from the MEALPY library. This column is simply meant to highlight similar algorithms which are not from the same group. We are interested in this, since similarities in algorithms belonging to different groups may be more difficult to detect if they are presented in terms of seemingly unrelated metaphors.
We can categorize the top 30 similar algorithms pairs into the following groups:

\textit{i)} Different implementation of the same algorithm, which are expected to be similar: This is the case for algorithms such as (BaseALO, OriginalALO) and (BaseSCA, OriginalSCA), where the "Base" version is an implementation of the original algorithm developed by the MEALPY library creators.

\textit{ii)} Algorithm pairs where one algorithm is a variant of the other, and are also expected to be similar: (HI\_WOA, OriginalWOA), (ImprovedBSO, OriginalBSO), (L\_SHADE, SHADE), (L\_SHADE, SADE),(SADE, SHADE), etc.

\textit{iii)} Algorithms that belong to the same group, but are based on a different metaphor. Such observed examples are the algorithms (OriginalFFA, OriginalSSpiderA) and (OriginalCSA, OriginalSSpiderA), (BaseMFO, OriginalABC) all of which both belong to the swarm group, however, the OriginalFFA is based on the flashing behavior of fireflies, OriginalSSpiderA is imitating spiders’ behaviors in nature, OriginalCSA is based on the breeding process of the cuckoo bird species, BaseMFO is based on the navigation method of moths, while the OriginalABC algorithm mimics the process of honey bees foraging for food.

\textit{iv)} Algorithms belonging to entirely different groups: (OriginalBSO, OriginalCSA), (ImprovedBSO, LevyTWO), (OriginalFPA, OriginalNMRA), (LevyTWO, OriginalCSA), (ImprovedBSO, OriginalSSpiderA), etc. These pairs combine algorithms with distinct optimization paradigms (e.g., math-based vs bio-based, physics-based vs evolutionary-based), making high similarity less intuitive. Further investigation into their behavior is required, perhaps using a manual component-by-component analysis~\cite{camacho_intelligent_water_drops}.


