\subsection{Discussion}
An important thing to keep in mind is that the analysis of algorithm search behavior can yield varying results depending on the specific configurations of the algorithms being compared. Different parameter settings, such as population size, mutation rates, and selection mechanisms, can substantially influence the search trajectories and the solutions explored by the algorithms. These variations can lead to different statistical characteristics in the populations analyzed, potentially altering the conclusions drawn about the similarity of the algorithms' search behaviors. However, for the purpose of this study, we have utilized the default configurations provided by the MEALPY library. This approach allows us to demonstrate the methodology and present initial findings without the added complexity of tuning each algorithm's parameters. By using default configurations, we ensure a standardized baseline for comparison, which is crucial for the reproducibility and initial validation of our proposed approach. Future work can extend this analysis by exploring the impact of different configurations on the search behavior.
Additionally, we have to point out that the crossmatch test is computationally expensive due to the fact that we are comparing populations at each iteration of the search process. Finally, we would like to address the fact that our methodology does not put an emphasis on the quality of the solutions obtained, but on the similarity of the produced trajectories. Therefore, the fact that two algorithms have a similar search behaviour may not always indicate that they explore the exact same solutions or that they have identical performance. The proposed statistical test can therefore be complemented by additional similarity measures considering different aspects of the algorithms' behaviour to obtain an all-encompassing view.

\end{comment}
\section{Conclusion}
In this study, we introduced a novel empricial technique for comparing the search trajectories of optimization algorithms. Our findings revealed substantial similarities between certain algorithm pairs, particularly those where multiple variants are derived from the same base algorithm.
More interestingly, our approach also revealed similarities between seemingly unrelated algorithms based on different metaphors or belonging to entirely different groups, where further investigation is needed.
This approach provides a valuable metric for comparing newly proposed metaheuristic algorithms against existing ones, allowing researchers to assess the degree of similarity in their behavior and encourages the development of algorithms that bring meaningful improvements. %, whether in terms of performance, novelty, or other metrics. We hope this method will serve as a benchmark for future studies, discouraging the proliferation of algorithms that do not contribute significant advances in the field.

