\begin{abstract}
The uncertainty of wireless communication poses significant challenges to platoon control performance. Aiming at alleviating the influence of non-ideal communication on the platoon system, this paper proposes a distributed and adaptive model predictive control (MPC) method. First of all, to deal with the transmission uncertainty caused by non-ideal communication, compensated data packets are customized for each vehicle. Then, an adaptive model predictive control method is proposed to balance the system response speed and tracking accuracy. Furthermore, to reduce the computational requirements of the vehicle platoon system, a predictive time-domain update strategy suitable for non-ideal communication was introduced. Finally, the sufficient conditions for ensuring the feasibility of the MPC algorithm and the stability of the closed-loop platoon control system are theoretically analyzed. The simulation results show that the proposed method significantly reduces the computing resource requirements for solving the optimization problem while ensuring satisfactory system performance.
\keywords{Vehicle Platoon, Model Predictive Control, Adaptive Prediction Horizon Update Scheme.}
\end{abstract}
%
\section{Introduction}
Traditionally, vehicles have relied on onboard sensors to measure the states of nearby vehicles.  With the advance of vehicle-to-everything (V2X) communication, vehicles are now capable of acquiring information beyond their direct sensing range, including data that cannot be detected by remote sensors (e.g., internal control commands issued by other vehicles). This enhanced information exchange facilitates improved control performance for vehicle platoons. However, it also introduces various communication challenges to the platoon system, such as network congestion and connection interruptions, resulting in non-ideal communication conditions. The most common manifestations of these are communication delays and data packet losses. When such delays and losses occur, the tracking performance of the platoon may deteriorate significantly, potentially compromising road safety and leading to substantial economic losses \cite{IOT4diubao}, \cite{IOT5shiyan}.

To address unreliable transmissions and ensure data integrity for vehicle platoon systems in non-ideal communication environments, various compensation methods have been proposed, such as the Smith predictor \cite{2023zhangg} and zero/hold input strategies \cite{Ge2021b}. However, suboptimal control input selection can degrade platoon performance. Distributed model predictive control (DMPC), which predicts future control sequences, offers active compensation for delays and packet loss, is particularly well-suited to complex communication systems \cite{D2011},\cite{Li2018}. Recent advances include DMPC frameworks with delay compensation \cite{Mwang2023} and compensators targeting V2X information loss \cite{Jliu2022}.

However, for high-dimensional systems with complex constraints and long prediction horizons, DMPC often involves significant computational complexity, making real-time optimization costly \cite{Sunzishiying},\cite{zishiying2}. Although various efforts have been devoted to addressing this issue, the standard DMPC framework typically employs a fixed prediction horizon, which keeps the computational load constant at each control update. As a result, high-complexity optimization problems must be solved even when the system state is already within the terminal region \cite{TAC11}. Thus, adaptive prediction horizons are necessary to further reduce computational burden. While variable horizon MPC methods have been proposed for discrete systems \cite{Sunzishiying} and continuous systems \cite{zishiying2}, these approaches are tailored for single-system scenarios and are not directly applicable to vehicle platoon control under non-ideal communication. Therefore, it is necessary to further investigate reducing computational complexity through adaptive prediction horizons.

Motivated by the above discussions, we develop distributed and adaptive MPC algorithms with variable prediction horizons for vehicles in a platoon. The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item An adaptive predictive horizon update scheme (APHUS) suitable for DMPC algorithm in non-ideal communication is proposed. This method effectively reduces the computational complexity of each optimization problem.
  \item To achieve improved tracking control performance, an adaptive MPC method is proposed, in which the weighting matrices are adjusted based on the state deviation to balance system response speed and tracking accuracy.
  \item By reasonably utilizing the predicted sequence, the impacts of non-ideal communication can be effectively mitigated. Sufficient conditions are established to ensure the iterative feasibility of the DMPC algorithms and the stability of the closed-loop platoon system. Furthermore, the feasibility and effectiveness of the algorithm are validated through simulations.
\end{itemize}

\begin{notations}\nonumber
Let $\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of integers, and $\mathbb{R}^n$ the $n$-dimensional real space. The integer interval $\{a, a+1, \ldots, b\}$ is abbreviated as $\mathbb{N}_{[a,b]}$. For a column vector $x$, $\|x\|$ denotes its Euclidean norm, while $\|x\|_P$ stands for its weighted norm with respect to a positive definite matrix $P$, i.e., $\|x\|_{P} = \sqrt{x^TPx}$. The cardinality of a set $X$ (where $X \subseteq \mathbb{N}_{[a,b]}$) is denoted as $\mathrm{card}(X)$. The largest and smallest absolute eigenvalues of a matrix $P$ are denoted by $\lambda_{\max}(P)$ and $\lambda_{\min}(P)$, respectively. For a subset $\mathbb{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, we set $\|\mathbb{X}\| =\sup_{x \in \mathbb{X}} \|x\|$.
\end{notations}

\section{System Model}
In this section, firstly, the longitudinal dynamics model of a single vehicle is presented. Subsequently, the non-ideal communication is modelled.

\subsection{Vehicle Longitudinal Dynamics Model}
The discrete-time vehicle longitudinal dynamics, similar form as \cite{chen2023}, \cite{Bian2022}, is given as:
\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{yuanshimoxing}
 \xi_{j}(k)=A_j\xi_{j}(k)+B_ju_{j}(k)+D\omega_{j}(k),
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}where $j \in \{1, 2, \ldots, M\}$. The state vector is given by $\xi_j(k) = [p_j(k),\, v_j(k),\, a_j(k)]^T$, where $p_j(k)$, $v_j(k)$, and $a_j(k)$ represent the position, velocity, and acceleration of the $j$th vehicle at time step $k$, respectively. $u_j$ denotes the desired input for the vehicle, and $n_j(k)$ is the additive disturbance. It is assumed that all vehicles share a uniform sampling interval $h$ and identical motor time constant $\tau$. The system matrices are as follows:

\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \
\begin{small}
$ A_j=\left[
  \begin{array}{c c c}
  1&h&0\\
0&1&h\\
0&0&1-\frac{h}{\tau}
  \end{array}
\right]$,
\end{small}
${\small B_j=\left[
  \begin{array}{c}
0\\
0\\
\frac{h}{\tau}
  \end{array}
\right]}$,
${\small D=\left[
  \begin{array}{c}
0\\
0\\
h
  \end{array}
\right]}$.

The normal dynamics of a vehicle corresponding to the system (\ref{yuanshimoxing}) is
\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{moxing1}
    \xi_{j}(k)=A_j\xi_{j}(k)+B_ju_{j}(k).
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}

The system states, control inputs, and external disturbances are subject to the following constraints:
\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{yueshufanwei}
\xi_{j}(k) \in \mathbb{X}_{j} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 1}, u_{j}(k) \in \mathbb{U}_{j} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{1}, \omega_i \in \mathbb{W}_{j} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 1}
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}where $\mathbb{X}_{j} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 1}$ and $\mathbb{U}_{j} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{1}$ denote compact and convex sets with the origins in their respective interior points, and the external disturbance is bounded by upper bound $\|\mathbb{W}_{j}\|$.

The discrete vehicle longitudinal error model can be expressed as:
\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{3moxing}
   {x}_{j}(k+1)={A}_j{x}_{j}(k)+{B}_j{u}_{j}(k)+{D}\omega_{j}(k),
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}where ${x}_{j}(k)=\xi_{j}(k)-\xi_{j}^{ref}(k)=[p_{j}(k)-p_{0}(k)+j\cdot d, v_{j}(k)-v_{0}(k), a_{j}(k)-a_{0}(k) ]$ represents the deviation between the actual state and the reference state. $d$ denotes the constant spacing  between the $j$th vehicle and its preceding $(j-1)$th vehicle. The nominal system corresponding to the system (\ref{yuanshimoxing}) is:
\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{biaochengmoxing1}
 \tilde{x}_{j}(k+1)={A}_j\tilde{x}_{j}(k)+{B}_j\tilde{u}_{j}(k)
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}


\subsection{Non-Ideal Communication Model}
Due to node failures and transmission errors, random packet loss and delay in platoon systems are inevitable. This subsection models communication delay and data packet loss respectively.
%Non-ideal communication environments may undermine the stability of the platoon and lead to serious traffic accidents, which greatly reduces the safety of vehicle control.

Communication delay can be expressed as a continuous Poisson process, so the delay follows an exponential distribution:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{3shiyan}
 f(\tau)=\lambda e^{(-\lambda \tau)}, 0 \leq \tau \leq \overline{\tau},
\end{eqnarray}
where $\tau$ represents the time delay, $\lambda$ is the rate parameter, which is the reciprocal of the average frequency of delay occurrence, and $\overline{\tau}$ is the maximum delay time.

On the other hand, to facilitate the recording of the duration of data packet loss, define $k_s^n$ as the start time of data packet loss and $t_s^n$ as the time when new data packets are received. Inspired by \cite{chen2023}, the model of packet loss is constructed based on duration, which is specifically expressed as:
\begin{eqnarray}
{l}\left( k \right) = \begin{cases}
{1},&k \in \bigcup {{\mathbb{N}_{\left[ {k_s^n,k_s^n + t_s^n} \right]}}},s \in \mathbb{N},   \\
{0},&\mathrm{{otherwise,}}
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray}
Here, $\rho \in (0,1)$ represents the proportion of the total non-ideal communication duration during this period. The variable $y(j)$ represents the delay indicator at the $j$th instant. When there is a delay, its value is 1; when there is no delay, its value is 0. \(\upsilon \geq 0\) is the upper limit of the duration of continuous non-ideal communication. Meanwhile, similar to \cite{chen2023}, the maximum duration of non-ideal communication is defined as $\bar{N}_b = \frac{\upsilon}{1 - \rho}$. It should be noted that this section only focuses on issues related to non-ideal communication between vehicles.

Finally, to detect non-ideal communication, each vehicle attaches a timestamp $t_k$ to its data packets. When receiving a neighbor's packet, the timestamp is checked and compared with the previous packet and the sampling period, enabling the identification of non-ideal communication intervals. If the received packet has the latest timestamp, the vehicle updates its information; otherwise, a non-latest timestamp indicates a delay. If the difference between timestamps exceeds the maximum allowable delay $\overline{\tau}$, the packet is considered lost \cite{Ge2023}. In both cases, the vehicle rejects the data packet and continues to use the previous predicted state sequence of the neighbor.
%Finally, to detect non-ideal communication situations, in this section, each vehicle will attach a timestamp $t_k$ to identify the data packets. When receiving the neighbor data packet information, the timestamp will be checked. By comparing the difference between the timestamp of the last received data packet and the timestamp of the currently received data packet, as well as the sampling period, it can be determined whether the communication channel is ideal within this time period. Specifically, when the timestamp of the received data packet is the latest, the data packet will be accepted, thereby updating the information of adjacent vehicles. If nearby vehicles find that the timestamp is not the latest, it indicates that there is a time delay. Specifically, if the difference between the two timestamps exceeds the maximum allowable delay $\overline{\tau}$, the data packet will be regarded as lost \cite{Ge2023}. In both cases, the received data packets will be rejected, and the vehicle will continue to use the previous state prediction sequence of the neighboring vehicle.

It is worth mentioning that the controller will solve the problem only when there is no non-ideal communication issue and the neighbor data packet is received. Therefore, we define $k_j^0$ to represent the initial instant, and $k_j^s$ to represent the $s$th ideal communication instant, that is, $s \in \mathbb{N}$.

\section{Design of Distributed and Adaptive MPC Algorithm}
This section firstly customizes compensation data packets for each vehicle to deal with the transmission uncertainty caused by non-ideal communication. Then, the system control objective is given, and the DMPC algorithm is designed for all vehicles in the platoon system.

\subsection{Construction of Compensation Data Packets}
For the convenience of subsequent analysis, the following three types of sequences are constructed:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i),\tilde{u}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i)$: Feasible prediction state sequence $\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i)$  obtained by (\ref{3moxing}) with feasible control input $\tilde{u}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i)$;
\item $x_{j}^*(k_{j}^s+i),u_{j}^*(k_{j}^s+i)$: Optimal state sequence $x_{j}^*(k_{j}^s+i)$ obtained by (\ref{3moxing}) with  optimal control input $u_{j}^*(k_{j}^s+i)$;
\item  $x_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s+i),u_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s+i)$: State trajectories $x_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s+i)$ for  transmission between vehicles and their neighbors, which are designed as:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{3chuandishuru}
&u_{j}^{BC}\left( {k_{j}^{s} + i|k_{j}^{s}} \right) =
\begin{cases}
{u_{j}^*\left( {k_{j}^{s} + i|k_{j}^s} \right)},&i \in  \mathbb{N}_{[0,N_j^s)}, \\
{K_{j}{\tilde{x}_{j}}\left( {k_{j}^{s} + i|k_{j}^s} \right)},&{i\in  \mathbb{N}_{[N_j^s,N_j^0)}},
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray}where $K_{j}$ is the controller gain of linear state feedback. Then $x_{j}^{BC}( {k_{j}^s + i|k_{j}^s} )$ can be obtained:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{3chuandizhuangtai}
&x_{j}^{BC}\left( {k_{j}^s + i|k_{j}^s} \right) =\begin{cases}
{x_{j}^{*}\left( {k_{j}^{s} + i|k_{j}^s} \right)},&i \in \mathbb{N}_{[1,N_j^s+1)},\\
{\left( {A_j + B_jK_{j}} \right)\tilde {x}_{j}\left( {k_{j}^s + N_j^s|k_{j}^s} \right)},&{i \in \mathbb{N}_{[N_j^s+1,N_j^0]}}.
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray}
\end{itemize}
%In this section, each vehicle solves its own optimization problem in parallel. Due to the existence of coupling constraints, vehicles cannot obtain the actual trajectories of surrounding vehicles in real time. Therefore, each vehicle will iteratively calculate its own predicted trajectory, and package the prediction results along with the corresponding timestamps into data packets, which are then transmitted to adjacent vehicles via wireless channels. Each vehicle needs to solve the optimization problem based on the current vehicle status and the status of neighboring vehicles. This control structure is applicable to all controlled vehicles.

To effectively solve the problem of insufficient data sources caused by the different prediction horizons of different vehicles, the data packets sent to adjacent vehicles are mainly divided into two parts: The first $N_j^s$ elements are calculated by the MPC controller in the prediction horizon of $N_j^s$, while the remaining $N_j^0-N_j^s$ elements are calculated by the feedback control gain $K_{j}$. It should be pointed out that the computational efficiency of iterative feedback control gain is very high.

\subsection{Control Objectives and Optimization Problems}
For the vehicle platoon control system, each vehicle needs to maintain a relatively safe distance from the vehicle in front and follow steadily to ensure that the vehicle platoon control maintains a certain formation. The control objective can be summarized as $\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}x_{j}(k_{j})=0$.

The objective functions of the optimization problem include state cost, synergy cost, control increment cost and terminal cost. Mathematically, the cost function of each vehicle can be expressed as:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}\label{3daijiahanshu2}
\begin{split}
  &J_{j}(\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}(k_{j}^s),\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}(k_{j}^s),\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}_{-j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s))\\
  =&\sum_{i=0}^{N_j^s-1}(\|\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\|_{Q_{j}}^2+\|\tilde{u}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\|_{R_{j}}^2+\sum_{q \in C_{j}}\|\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)-\tilde{x}_{q,j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\|_{Q_{jq}}^2)\\
  &+\| {\tilde x_{j}( {k_{j}^s + N_j^{s}|k_{j}^s})} \|_{{P_{j}}}^2,\\
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{small}where $\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}_j(k_j^s) = {\{{\tilde x}_j(k_j^{s}|k_j^{s}), \cdots, {\tilde x}_j(k_j^{s}+N_j^{s}|k_j^{s})\}}$, ${\boldsymbol{x}}^{BC}_{-j}(k_j^s)=\{x_{q,j}^{BC}(k_j^s),q \in C_j\}$, $\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}_j(k_j^s) = {\{{\tilde u}_j(k_j^{s}|k_j^{s}), \cdots, {\tilde u}_j(k_j^{s}+N_j^{s}-1|k_j^{s})   \}}$. $Q_j$, $R_j$, $Q_{jq}$, and $P_j $ are weighted matrices. $\|\tilde{x}_j(k_j^s+i|k_j^s)\|_{Q_j}^2$ is the state cost, which represents the error between the vehicle state and the reference trajectory. $\|\tilde{u}_j(k_j^s+i|k_j^s)\|_{R_j}^2$ is the incremental cost of control, indicating achievable acceleration and deceleration movements. $\sum_{q \in C_j}\|\tilde{x}_j(k_j^s+i|k_j^s)-\tilde{x}_{q,j}^{BC}(k_j^s)\|_{Q_{jq}}^2$ stands for that the vehicle  should follow the assumed state of its neighbors as much as possible to achieve consistency. $\| {\tilde x_j( {k_j^s + N_j^{s}|k_j^s} )}\|_{{P_j}}^2 $ is the terminal cost.

In summary, the MPC optimization problem can be formulated in the following form:
\begin{small}
\begin{subequations}\label{3wenti1}
\begin{align}
&\min_{\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}(k_{j}^s)}\notag\   J_{j}(\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}(k_{j}^s),\tilde{\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}(k_{j}^s),{\boldsymbol{x}}_{-j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s))\\
\text{s.t.}\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ &\mathrm{for}\  i \in \mathbb{N}_{[0,{N}_{j}^s-1]},\notag\\
&\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i+1|k_{j}^s)=A\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)
+B\tilde{u}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s),\label{3wenti1:1}\\
  &\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s) \leq (1-\varrho_{j}\frac{i}{N_j^s})\|\mathbb{X}_{j}\|,\label{3wenti1:2}\\
  &u_{min}\leq \tilde{u}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\leq u_{max},\label{3wenti1:3}\\
  &\Delta u_{min}\leq\Delta \tilde{u}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\leq  \Delta u_{max},\label{3wenti1:4}\\
  &\!\sum\limits_{q \in {C_{j}}}\|\tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)-\tilde{x}_{q,j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\|_{Q_{jq}}^2 \leq card(C_{j})\zeta\!,\label{3wenti1:5} \\
& \| \tilde{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s+{N}_{s}^j|k_{j}^s) \|_{{P_{j}}}^2 \leq \varepsilon_j^2,\label{3wenti1:6}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
\end{small}where (\ref{3wenti1:1}) and (\ref{3wenti1:2}) represent the state constraint and robustness constraint, respectively. (\ref{3wenti1:3}) and (\ref{3wenti1:4}) respectively give the input constraint and the input increment constraint. (\ref{3wenti1:5}) is the neighbor consistency constraint, and (\ref{3wenti1:6}) is the terminal constraint. $\varrho_ {j} \in (0, 1) $ represents scaling parameter, $u_ {min} $, $u_ {max} $, $\Delta u_ {min} $ and $\Delta u_ {max} $ are input and input increment physical limits. $card(C_{j})$ represents the cardinality of the set $C_{j}$, and $\zeta$ is the consistency constraint parameter. In addition, $\varepsilon_{j} \in (0,\gamma_j)$ is a constant which is employed for the specification of the terminal constraint.

After solving the above optimization problem, the optimal control sequence ${\boldsymbol{u}}^*_{j}(k_{j}^s)\!\!=\!\!\{{u}_{j}^*(k_{j}^{s}|k_{j}^{s}), \dots, {u}_{j}^*(k_{j}^{s}\!+\!{N}_{s}^j\!-\!1|k_{j}^{s})\}$ and the corresponding state sequence ${\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}^*(k_{j}^s) \!\!=\!\!\{{x}_{j}^*(k_{j}^{s}|k_{j}^{s}), \dots, {x}_{j}^*(k_{j}^{s}\!+\!{N}_{s}^j|k_{j}^{s})\}$ are obtained. For each vehicle, the control input sequence ${\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s)\!\!=\!\!\{{u}_{j}^{BC}\!(0|k_{j}^{s}), {u}_{j}^{BC}\!(1|k_{j}^{s}), \dots\}$ and the state packet sequence ${\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s)\!=\!\{{x}_{j}^{BC}\!(0|k_{j}^{s}), {x}_{j}^{BC}\!(1|k_{j}^{s}), \dots\}$ are defined for transmission. %These sequences are ready to be sent after the MPC problem is solved.


\subsection{Adaptive Weighting Coefficient}
In the cost function (\ref{3daijiahanshu2}), most weighting matrices are set as diagonal matrices. The state weighting matrix primarily reflects the system’s trajectory tracking capability, while the control increment weighting matrix reflects the requirement for smooth control actions. Let $Q_j = q_j \cdot \mathbb{I}$ and $R_j = r_j \cdot \mathbb{I}$, and the cooperative cost weighting is set as $Q_{jq} = 0.5 \cdot Q_j$. Considering that the system may fail to track the reference signal satisfactorily in the event of abrupt changes, this subsection proposes the use of adaptive weighting parameters.

Let $\Xi_j > 0$ denote the allowable state deviation. When the state deviation is small, i.e., $\frac{\|x_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)-{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s) \|_{P_j}}{\Xi_j }<1$, the focus should be on the smoothness requirement of the control signal. The weight update rule is defined as:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
q_j = q_j^0 - \frac{e^b \|x_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)-{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s) \|_{P_j}}{\Xi_j } \\
r_j = r_j^0
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{small}Conversely, when the state deviation is large, i.e., $\frac{\|x_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)-{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s) \|_{P_j}}{\Xi_j } \geq 1$, rapid trajectory tracking becomes the priority, and the weight update rule is:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
 q_j = q_j^1 \\
 r_j = r_j^1 - \frac{e^c\|x_{j}(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)-{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s) \|_{P_j}}{\Xi_j }
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{small}Here, $e$ is the natural exponential constant, $q_j^0$, $r_j^0$, $q_j^1$ and $r_j^1$ denote tunable weighting coefficient values, and $b$, $c$ are positive integer parameters.

With these adaptive parameters, the system can quickly adjust the primary control objectives. Specifically, when the system state deviation is less than $\Xi_j$, the aforementioned update rule decreases $q_j$, resulting in a higher cost for control increment and thus smoother control signals. Conversely, when the state deviation exceeds $\Xi_j$, $r_j$ decreases, assigning a higher cost to state deviation and enabling the input signal to change rapidly for fast tracking. In accordance with the changes in system state, the weighting coefficients can be flexibly adjusted, thereby ensuring good tracking performance of the vehicle.


\subsection{Adaptive Predictive Horizon Update Strategy}

In this section, we propose an APHUS for MPC under imperfect communication conditions, formulated as follows:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}\label{3yuceshiyu}
N_j^{s+1}\!=\!\min \Bigg\{\hat{N}_j^s,\, N_j^{s}\!+\!k_{j}^s - k_{j}^{s+1}\!+\!1,\, \frac{\lambda_{\min}\left(P_{r,i}\right)(\gamma_j^2\!-\! \varepsilon_j^2)}{\lambda_{\max}\left(Q_{r,i}\!+\!K_{r,i}^T R_{r,i} K_{r,i}\right) \epsilon_j^2}\!+\!1,\, \bar{N}_b \Bigg\}
\end{equation}
\end{small}
Here, the optimal predictive horizon $\hat{N}_j^s\!=\!\inf\!\left\{i:\!\tilde{x}^*_j(k_{j}^s\!+\!i | k_{j}^s)\!\in\!\mathbb{X}_\varepsilon,\, i\!\in\!\mathbb{N}_{[0, N_j^s\!-\!1]} \right\}$ is determined by solving the optimization problem in (\ref{3wenti1}) online, and represents the shortest predictive horizon that ensures system stability. The condition $N_j^{s\!+\!1}\!\!\geq\!\!N_j^{s}\!+\!k_{j}^{s}\!-\!k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}\!+\!1$ implies $N_j^{s\!+\!1}\!+\!k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}\!\!\geq\!\!N_j^{s}\!+\!k_{j}^{s}\!+\!1$, $N_j^{s\!+\!1}\!\!\geq\!\!\frac{\lambda_{\min}\left(P_{r,i}\right)(\gamma_j^2\!-\!\varepsilon_j^2)}{\lambda_{\max}\left(Q_{r,i}\!+\!K_{r,i}^T R_{r,i} K_{r,i}\right)\epsilon_j^2}\!+\!1$ ensures that the predicted state will eventually converge to the terminal constraint, and $N_j^{s\!+\!1}\!\!\geq\!\!\bar{N}_b$ guarantees that, under imperfect communication, the predicted sequence provides a minimum compensation for effective data.

Let $N_j^0$ denote the initial predictive horizon for the $j$th vehicle. For simplicity, it is assumed that $N_j^0$ is identical for all vehicles. Accordingly, the subsequent predictive horizons $N_j^{s+1}$ can be derived using (\ref{3yuceshiyu}). It is worth noting that, due to abrupt changes in the reference trajectory, the system state $x(k_j^s)$ may deviate from the terminal constraint set. At this instant, it is necessary to update the predictive horizon and re-compute the control gains, i.e., initialize the predictive horizon to $N_j^0$ and resolve the optimization problem.
\begin{assumption}
For the vehicle platoon system described by (\ref{3moxing}), selecting an appropriate prediction horizon $N_j^0$ guarantees that the optimization problem in (\ref{3wenti1}) is feasible.
\end{assumption}


\section{Feasibility and Stability Analysis} \label{section:D}
This section investigates the feasibility and stability of the MPC algorithm with adaptive weight parameters. Firstly, the recursive feasibility conditions are established. Then, the input-to-state stability of the system is analyzed in detail.

To facilitate the subsequent analysis, the commonly used assumptions and lemmas from the MPC literature which are essential for constructing terminal control laws and terminal sets are introduced \cite{H1998}.
\begin{assumption}
Consider the nominal deviation system presented in (\ref{biaochengmoxing1}). There exists a state feedback controller of the form ${\small \tilde{u}_{j}(k)=K_{j}\tilde{x}_{j}(k)}$ such that the resulting closed-loop dynamics are described by {\small $\tilde{x}_{j}(k+1)=A_{j,cl} \tilde{x}_{j}(k)$}, where ${\small A_{j,cl}=A_j+B_jK_{j}}$.
\end{assumption}
\begin{lemma}\label{3yinli1}
Suppose that $Q_{j}$ and $R_{j}$ are positive definite matrices. Then, there exists a matrix $P_{j}$, such that: (1) the set {\small $\Omega_{j} = \left\{\tilde{x} : \| \tilde{x}_{j}(k) \|_{P_{j}}^2 \leq \gamma_j^2 \right\}$} remains invariant for the system {\small $\tilde{x}_{j}(k+1)=A_{j,cl} \tilde{x}_{j}(k)$}; and (2) for any $\tilde{x}_{j}(k) \in \Omega_{j}$, the control law ensures the input constraints are satisfied and the condition $\| \tilde{x}_{j}(k+1) \|_{P_{j}}^2 - \| \tilde{x}_{j}(k) \|_{P_{j}}^2 \leq -\| \tilde{x}_{j}(k)\|_{\phi_j}^2$ holds, where $\phi_j = Q_{j} + K_{j}^{T} P_{j} K_{j}$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{Iterative Feasibility of MPC}
Initially, the first optimal control quantity of ${\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s)$ is used to control the actual system over time periods $k_{j}^{s}$ to $k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}$. Thus, the candidate control sequence and state sequence are constructed as $\hat {\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}\!(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\!=\!{\{{\hat u}_{j}(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}\!|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}),\!\cdots\!, {\hat u}_{j}\!(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}\!\!+\!\!N_j^s\!\!-\!\!1\!|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\!\}}$ and $\hat {\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\!=\!{\{{\hat x}_{j}(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}), \cdots, {\hat x}_{j}(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}\!+\!N_j^s\!-\!1|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\!\}}$.

The candidate input can be established by shifting the control sequence in equation (\ref{3chuandishuru}) by $k_{j}^{s + 1} - k_{j}^{s} $ iterations as:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{3houxuanshuru}
\begin{split}
&\hat u_{j}( {k_{j}^{s + 1} +i|k_{j}^{s + 1}} ) = \begin{cases}
{u_{j}^{BC}( {k_{j}^{s + 1} + i|k_{j}^s} )},&i \in  \mathbb{N}_{[0,{N_j^s} + k_{j}^s - k_{j}^{s + 1}]},\\
{K_{j}{x_{j}^{BC}}( {k_{j}^{s + 1} + i|k_{j}^s} )}, &{i\in \mathbb{N}_{[{{N_j^s} + k_{j}^s - k_{j}^{s + 1}+1},{N_j^s} + k_{j}^{s + 1})}.}
\end{cases}
\end{split}
\end{eqnarray}
At the next trigger instant $k_{j}^{s + 1}$, the neighboring information $\hat {\boldsymbol{x}}_{-j}^{BC}(k_{j}^{s + 1})$ can be determined in a comparable way by utilizing equation (\ref{3houxuanshuru})  for $q \in C_{j}$.
\begin{theorem}
Suppose that Assumptions 1, 2 hold. Then, under the proposed adaptive weight parameter update and APHUS, the sufficient condition for the recursive feasibility of the optimization problem (\ref{3wenti1}) is:
\begin{equation}\label{3kexing1}
\begin{split}
\delta_j^{\text{max}} \leq \min\{\frac{\gamma_j -\varepsilon_j}{\lambda_{\text{max}}(\sqrt{P_j})}, \frac{\varrho_{j}\|\mathbb{X}_{j}\|}{N_j^s}\}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\delta_j^{\text{max}}=\|DW_j\|\bar{N}_b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given the definition of candidate input in (\ref{3houxuanshuru}), the control input constraints, input incremental constraints, and neighbor consistency constraints are satisfied at $k_j^s$. To demonstrate recursive feasibility at each sampling time sequence $\{k_{j}^{s}, k_{j}^{s+1}, \cdots \}$, it is necessary to show that at time $k_{j}^{s+1}$, the candidate state generated by (\ref{3houxuanshuru}) satisfies the robustness constraint in (\ref{3wenti1:2}) and the terminal constraint in (\ref{3wenti1:6}).

First, we prove that the candidate state $\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{j}(k_{j}^{s + 1})$ satisfies both the robustness and terminal constraints for $i \in \mathbb{N}_{[0,N_j^{s}-k_{j}^{s+1}+k_{j}^{s}]}$. According to the model iteration rule, the following inequality can be obtained
$\| \hat{x}_{j}(k_{j}^{s + 1}+i | k_{j}^{s + 1}) - x_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^{s + 1}+i | k_{j}^{s}) \| \leq \lambda_{\text{max}}(A_j^i)\delta_j^{\text{max}} \leq \delta_j^{\text{max}}, \quad i \in \mathbb{N}_{[0,N_j^{s}]}
\ $ Then, applying the triangle inequality yields $
\| \hat{x}_{j}(k_{j}^{s + 1}+i | k_{j}^{s + 1}) \| \le \| x_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^{s+1}+i | k_{j}^s) \| + \delta_j^{\text{max}} \le (1-\varrho_{j}(\frac{j}{N_j^{s}}))\|\mathbb{X}_{j}\|$. Since $\delta_j^{\text{max}}$ satisfies (\ref{3kexing1}), we have the following inequality:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}\label{3kexing11}
\| \hat{x}_{j}(k_{j}^{s + 1}\!+\!i | k_{j}^{s + 1}) \|\!\leq\!\left(1\!-\!\varrho_{j}\left(\frac{k_{j}^{s+1}\!-\!k_{j}^{s}\!+\!i}{N_j^{s}}\right)\right)\|\mathbb{X}_{j}\|
\end{equation}
\end{small}

To prove the inequality $\| \hat{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s\!+\!N_j^{s}|k_{j}^{s+1})\!\|_{P_{j}}^2\!\!\le\!\!\gamma_j^2$, the following relationship needs to be derived $
\| \hat{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s\!+\!N_j^{s}\!| k_{j}^{s+1})\!\|_{P_{j}}\!\!\le\!\!\| x_{j}^{BC}(k_{j}^s\!+\!N_j^{s} | k_{j}^s )\!\|_{P_{j}}\!+\!\lambda_{\text{max}}\!(\sqrt{P_{j}} )\delta_j^{\text{max}}\!\!\le\!\!\gamma_j$. According to the definition of the optimization problem (\ref{3wenti1}) at time $k_{j}^s$, we have $\|x_{j}(k_{j}^s\!+\!N_j^{s}|\!k_{j}^s) \|_{P_{j}}\!\!\leq\!\!\varepsilon_j$. Combining these inequalities yields:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}\label{3tiaojian1}
\lambda_{\text{max}} \left( \sqrt{P_{j}} \right) \delta_j^{\text{max}} \le \gamma_j - \varepsilon_j.
\end{equation}
\end{small}From (\ref{3kexing11}) and (\ref{3tiaojian1}), condition (\ref{3kexing1}) follows, which means that the robustness constraint is satisfied for $i\!\in\! \mathbb{N}_{[0,N_j^s-k_{j}^{s+1}+k_{j}^{s}]}$, and $\|\!\hat{x}_{j}(k_{j}^s\!+\!N_j^s | k_{j}^{s+1}) \|_{P_{j}}^2\!\!\le\!\!\gamma_j^2$ holds.

Next, we prove that when $i=N_j^s$, the predicted state will ultimately converge to the terminal constraint $\varepsilon_{j}$. According to Lemma \ref{3yinli1}, for $i\!\in\!\! \mathbb{N}_{[0, k_{j}^{s+1}-k_{j}^{s}]}$, we have:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}\label{tiaojiannnn}
&\left\| \hat{x}_{j}\left( k_{j}^s + N_j^{s} + i + 1 | k_{j}^{s + 1} \right) \right\|_{P_{j}}^2 - \left\| \hat{x}_{j}\left( k_{j}^s + N_j^{s} + i | k_{j}^{s + 1} \right) \right\|_{P_{j}}^2\\
= & \left\| \hat{x}_{j}\left( k_{j}^s + N_j^{s} + i | k_{j}^{s + 1} \right) \right\|_{\left( A_j + B_j K_{j} \right)^T P_{j} \left( A_j + B_j K_{j} \right) - P_{j}}^2\\
\le &  - \left\| \hat{x}_{j}\left( k_{j}^s + N_j^{s} + i | k_{j}^{s + 1} \right) \right\|_{Q_{j} + K_{j}^T R_{j} K_{j}}^2 \le - \left\| \hat{x}_{j}\left( k_{j}^s + N_j^{s} | k_{j}^{s + 1} \right) \right\|_{Q_{j} + K_{j}^T R_{j} K_{j}}^2.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{small}According to the specified APHUS rule, it is easy to see that within this variable prediction horizon, the system's final terminal state successfully enters the terminal constraint region.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Stability Analysis}

\begin{theorem}
Consider system (\ref{3moxing}) under the controller (\ref{3wenti1}). If inequality (\ref{3kexing1}) holds for any given $x_{j} \in {\mathbb{X}_{j}}$, then the vehicle platoon system is input-to-state practically stable (ISpS).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the platoon control system, the Lyapunov function is represented as the sum of the optimal cost functions of all vehicles. Mathematically, it is given by
 $V\!({k_{j}^{s+1}})\!-\!V\!({k_{j}^s})\!=\!\sum\limits_{i\!=\!1}^{M}(\!J_{j}(x_j\!(k_j^{s+1}),{\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}^*\!(k_{j}^{s+1}),{\boldsymbol{x}}_{-j}^{BC}\!(k_{j}^{s+1}))\!-\! J_{j}(x_j(k_j^{s}),{\boldsymbol{u}}_{j}^*(k_{j}^{s}),{\boldsymbol{x}}_{-j}^{BC}(k_{j}^{s})))$. At each sampling instant $k_{j}^s$, the optimization problem (\ref{3wenti1}) is solved to obtain the optimal control sequence $\boldsymbol{u}_j^*(k_{j}^s)$. Based on (\ref{3houxuanshuru}), a feasible sequence is obtained. The difference in the Lyapunov function between two consecutive sampling instants satisfies $V(k_{j}^{s+1}) - V(k_{j}^s) = \sum_{i=1}^{M} (\Delta J_1 + \Delta J_2 + \Delta J_3 + \Delta J_4)$, where $\Delta J_1$, $\Delta J_2$, $\Delta J_3$, and $\Delta J_4$ are given as follows:
\begin{small}
\begin{align*}
\Delta J_1\!\!=&\!\!-\!\!\!\!\sum_{i\!=\!N_j^{s}}^{N_j^{s\!+\!1}\!-\!1}\!\!\!\!(\|\tilde{x}_j(k_{j}^s\!\!+\!\!i|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\|_{Q_j}^2\!\!+\!\!\|\tilde{u}_j(k_{j}^s\!\!+\!\!i|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\|_{R_j}^2 )\!\!+\!\!\|\tilde{x}_j(k_{j}^{s\!+\!1}\!\!+\!\!N_j^{s\!+\!1}|k_{j}^{s\!+\!1})\|_{P_j}^2\!\!-\!\!\|\tilde{x}_j^*(k_{j}^s\!\!+\!\!N_j^{s}|k_{j}^s)\!\|_{P_j}^2,\\
\Delta J_2\!\!=& -\sum_{i=0}^{k_{j}^{s+1}-k_{j}^s} ( \|\tilde{x}_j(k_{j}^s+i|k_{j}^s)\|_{Q_j}^2 + \|\tilde{u}_j(k_{j}^s+j|k_{j}^s)\|_{R_j}^2 ), \\
\Delta J_3\!\!=& \sum_{i=k_j^{s+1}}^{k_{j}^s+N_j^{s}} (\|\tilde{x}_j(i|k_j^{s+1})\|_{Q_j}^2-\|\tilde{x}_j^*(i|k_j^{s})\|_{Q_j}^2),\\
\Delta J_4\!\!=&\!\!\sum_{i = 0}^{N_j^{s}-1}\!\!\sum_{q \in C_j}(\left \| {x}_j(k_j^{s+1}\!\!+\!\!i|k_j^{s+1})\!\!-\!\!x_{q,j}^{BC}(k_j^{s+1}\!\!+\!\!i|k_j^{s+1})  \right \|\!\!-\!\!\left \| {x}_j^*(k_j^{s\!+\!1}\!\!+\!\!i|k_j^{s})\!\!-\!\!x_{q,j}^{BC*}(k_j^{s\!+\!1}\!\!+\!\!i|k_j^{s\!+\!1})  \right \|)\\
&+\sum_{i=k_j^s+N_j^{s}}^{k_j^{s+1}+N_j^{s+1}-1} \sum_{q \in C_j}  \left \| {x}_j(k_j^{s+1}+i|k_j^{s+1})-x_{q,j}^{BC}(k_j^{s+1}+i|k_j^{s+1})\right \|\\
&-\sum_{i = 0}^{k_{j}^{s+1}-k_j^s-1} \sum_{q \in C_j}\left \| {x}_j^*(k_j^{s}+i|k_j^{s})-x_{q,j}^{BC}(k_j^{s}+i|k_j^{s})  \right \|.
\end{align*}
\end{small}
From Lemma 1 and (\ref{3wenti1:6}), by using trigonometric inequalities, it is clear that:
\begin{small}
\begin{align}\label{zongjie1}
\Delta J_1\leq& \|\tilde{x}_j(k_j^s+N_j^s|k_j^{s+1})\|_{P_j}^2-\|\tilde{x}_j^*(k_j^s+N_j^s|k_j^{s})\|_{P_j}^2\notag\\
\leq & (\|\tilde{x}_j(k_j^s+N_j^s|k_j^{s+1})\|_{P_j}- \|\tilde{x}_j^*(k_j^s+N_j^s|k_j^{s}) \|_{P_j})\notag\\
&\times(\|\tilde{x}_j(k_j^s+N_j^s|k_j^{s+1})\|_{P_j}+ \|\tilde{x}_j^*(k_j^s+N_j^s|k_j^{s}) \|_{P_j})\notag\\
\leq& \lambda_{\text{max}}(A_j^{N_j^{s}})  \delta_j^{\text{max}}(\gamma_j +\varepsilon_j) =\delta_j^{\text{max}}(\gamma_j +\varepsilon_j).
\end{align}
\end{small}
From (\ref{3wenti1:2}), By using trigonometric inequalities, it follows that:
\begin{small}
\begin{align}\label{zongjie2}
\Delta J_3 &\leq \sum_{i=k_j^{s+1}}^{k_j^{s}+N_j^{s}} (\|\tilde{x}_{j}(i|k_j^{s+1})\|_{Q_j}-\|\tilde{x}^*_{j}(i|k_j^{s})\|_{Q_j})(\|\tilde{x}_{j}(i|k_j^{s+1})\|_{Q_j}+\|\tilde{x}^*_{j}(i|k_j^{s})\|_{Q_j})\notag\\
&\leq \frac{\lambda_{\text{max}}(Q_j)}{\lambda_{\text{min}}(P_j) } \sum_{i=k_j^{s+1}}^{k_j^{s}+N_j^{s}}(\delta_j^{\text{max}})(\delta_j^{\text{max}}+2 \cdot (1-\frac{i}{N_j^s}\cdot \varrho_{j})\|\mathbb{X}_{j}\|).
\end{align}
\end{small}
For collaborative costs, by using trigonometric inequalities, we further obtain:

\begin{small}
\begin{equation}\label{sanjiaobudengshi11}
\begin{split}
&\| {{x_{j}}( {j|k_{j}^{s + 1}} ) - x_{q,j}^{BC}( {j|k_{j}^{s + 1}} )} \|_{{Q_{jq}}}^2 - \| {x_{j}^*( {j|k_{j}^s} ) - x_{q,j}^{BC*}( {j|k_{j}^{s + 1}} )} \|_{{Q_{jq}}}^2\\
\le& \| {{x_{j}}( {j|k_{j}^{s + 1}} ) - x_{j}^*( {j|k_{j}^s} )} \|_{{Q_{jq}}}^2+ \| {x_{q,j}^{BC*}( {j|k_{j}^{s + 1}} ) - x_{q,j}^{BC}( {j|k_{j}^{s + 1}} )} \|_{{Q_{jq}}}^2.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{small}
In the light of (\ref{3wenti1:5}) and (\ref{sanjiaobudengshi11}), we can obtain:
\begin{small}
\begin{align}\label{zongjie3}
\Delta J_4 \leq& \sum_{q \in C_j} \lambda_{\text{max}}(Q_{jq})(N_j^{s}-1)(\delta_j^{\text{max}}+\delta_{\text{max}}^q)+{N_j^s \cdot card( {{C_{j}}} )\frac{{{\lambda _{\max}}( {{Q_{jq}}} )}}{{{\lambda _{\min }}( {{P_{j}}} )}}\gamma_j^2}.
\end{align}
\end{small}
Ultimately, one derives:
\begin{small}
\begin{equation}\label{zongjie4}
\begin{split}
& {\Delta J_1}+{\Delta J_2}+{\Delta J_3}+{\Delta J_4} \le -\overline {a}(\| {x_{j}({k_{j}^s})} \|)+\overline {r} (\| {\mathbb{W}_{j}} \|)+\overline{c},
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{small}where the forms of $\overline {a}, \overline {r}$ and $\overline {c}$ can be obtained from (\ref{zongjie1}), (\ref{zongjie2}) and (\ref{zongjie3}), It is easily deduced that $V({k_{j}^s})$ has lower and upper bounds delineated by two distinct $L \infty$ functions. Consequently, $V({k_{j}^s})$ qualifies as an ISpS-Lyapunov function within the closed-loop configuration. Hence, the stability of the closed-loop structure is assured to be ISpS.
\end{proof}

\section{Simulation and Discussion}\label{section:E}
The proposed algorithm was validated in MATLAB/Simulink. Simulation parameters were first provided, followed by tests under non-ideal communication conditions to assess algorithm robustness. Finally, comparisons with MPC without adaptive parameters and conventional MPC are presented. %demonstrated that the proposed method achieves stable tracking and improved real-time performance while reducing computation time in non-ideal scenarios.

\subsection{Simulation Parameter Settings}
We choose the number of platoon members $M$ being 3, set the initial cruise speed of the platoon to be $25 m/s$ and the initial acceleration $0 m/s^2$. Considering the speed variation of overtaking at high speeds, the reference velocity changes to be $30m/s$ at 8s. To better simulate real-world scenarios, the lead vehicle follows a step reference, while each following vehicle tracks the actual trajectory of the lead vehicle. The motor time constant is set to $\tau_j = 0.5\,\mathrm{s}$, and the sampling period to $h = 0.05\,\mathrm{s}$. The desired inter-vehicle distance is $d = 10\,\mathrm{m}$, with the maximum additive disturbance magnitude of 0.2. For simplicity, the initial prediction horizon for all vehicles is uniformly set to $N_j^0 = 30$. The feedback gain $K_j$ is chosen as the linear quadratic optimal gain, and the terminal weighting matrix $P_j$ is obtained by solving the Riccati equation. The total simulation time in Simulink, $t_{\mathrm{all}}$, is set to 30s.
