\begin{abstract}
    % Advances in scientific knowledge have historically been often earned through labour-intensive trial and error, the process that is slow, expensive, and thus inaccessible to many.
    % Increased computation capabilities, digitalization, and machine learning have recently created opportunities for new tools which make science faster, cheaper, and available to a broader range of people can benefit society as a whole, %Probably should tone this down a bit
    % and many already exist for specialised tasks. Virtual Laboratories (VLs) represent a paradigm shift in scientific research, seamlessly bridging the divide between the physical and digital realms a wide range of benefits.
    % We propose a design of Virtual AI-assisted Laboratories (VAILabs), a concept which reproduces much of the physical scientific and experimental process in the digital space and leverages AI techniques to act as an assistant to this process.
    % VAILabs is a modular software library that can be used as a workbench and toolbox for endeavours embracing the virtual lab approach.
    % This work outlines general design principles for such a tool and demonstrates a proof-of-concept that can be used for building custom pipelines for designing and running experiments using the AI-assistant as a partner in this process.
    % By using high-fidelity digital analogues for physical processes to take advantage of rapid computing and making these tools open source, science can be made more reachable than it ever was. 

    Many scientific disciplines have traditionally advanced by iterating over hypotheses using labor-intensive trial-and-error, which is a slow and expensive process. Recent advances in computing, digitalization, and machine learning have introduced tools that promise to make scientific research faster by assisting in this iterative process. However, these advances are scattered across disciplines and only loosely connected, with specific computational methods being primarily developed for narrow domain-specific applications. 
    %very limited efforts still dedicated to how to best use the different tools within the scientific discovery process.
    Virtual Laboratories are being proposed as a unified formulation 
    % on how to assist the researchers in utilizing the increasingly more digital environment, through common AI technologies, but the implementations still seek their form.
    to help researchers navigate this increasingly digital landscape using common AI technologies. While conceptually promising, VLs are not yet widely adopted in practice, and concrete implementations remain limited.
    %Virtual Laboratories represent a significant shift in scientific research by bridging the physical and digital realms. This paper proposes the design of Virtual AI-assisted Laboratories (VAILabs), which replicate physical scientific and experimental processes in a digital space with AI assistance. 
    This paper explains how the Virtual Laboratory concept can be implemented in practice by introducing the modular software library 
    VAILabs, designed to support scientific discovery. VAILabs provides a flexible workbench and toolbox for a broad range of scientific domains. We outline the design principles and demonstrate a proof-of-concept by mapping three concrete research tasks from differing fields as virtual laboratory workflows. 
    %By utilising high-fidelity digital analogues for physical processes to take advantage of rapid computing and making these tools open source, science can be made more reachable than it ever was. 
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Virtual Laboratories, ML-Assisted Research, Automated Experimental Design, Workflow Manager}


