\section{Introduction}
    % \subsection{The challenge}        
        Scientific research is often a slow process, requiring repeated examination of alternative hypotheses in search of new knowledge. In most cases, this is labor-intensive and costly, for instance, due to slow laboratory experimentation and complex processes.
        Digital tools offer transformative potential for increasing research efficiency. Among them are computational simulations and machine learning proxies, capable of predicting outcomes without manual experimentation \cite{wang2022inverse}. Others include co-researchers based on large language models (LLMs), which can act as discussion partners or perform sub-tasks on behalf of the researcher \cite{schmidgall2025agentlab,swanson2024virtual}.
        % Digital tools offer transformative potential in increased efficiency, ranging from computational simulations and machine learning proxies -- that provide insights on possible outcomes without requiring manual experimentation \cite{wang2022inverse} -- to co-researchers built from large language models (LLM) -- that work as discussion partners or even perform sub-tasks on behalf of the researcher \cite{schmidgall2025agentlab,swanson2024virtual}. 
        % In some cases the generative models are even in charge of the process; in a recent example \cite{swanson2024virtual} validated SARS-CoV-2 nanobodies using a constellation of a LLM investigator and collection of complementary AI agents.
        % One recent example is the Virtual Lab framework, which manages an entire research cycle by assembling specialist agents (chemist, structural biologist, critic) under an LLM principal investigator to design and experimentally validate SARS-CoV-2 nanobodies \cite{swanson2024virtual}.
        For example, in drug discovery, computational simulations have long provided valuable preliminary insights \cite{sliwoski2014computational}, generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) methods are used to propose novel candidates \cite{blaschke2020reinvent}, and LLMs are revolutionizing the ability to use historical information stored in scientific publications \cite{li2025scilit}. Nevertheless, the experimental validation of a drug candidate's efficiency remains essential. That is, the computational tools \emph{complement} rather than replace physical experiments, offering partial information that can guide subsequent testing steps. These tools have been integrated into individual research steps or short sequences of steps across various experimental science domains. However, comprehensive and unified strategies for designing entire workflows are still taking shape. Such strategies must be efficient, avoiding the need to involve a prohibitively large number of laboratory scientists in redesigning the workflow process every time the research focus shifts. As we integrate these digital tools, a key challenge indeed emerges: we need robust methodologies to help researchers understand when and how these tools should be applied to maximize their potential impact.

        % For example, in drug discovery, an experiment that validates the efficacy of a new drug candidate could be replaced by a computational simulation \cite{sliwoski2014computational}. 
        %Moreover, while digitisation, combined with the computational power of Machine Learning (ML), allows us to extend the boundaries of traditional experimental methods, the true challenge lies in optimizing how we use these models. Much of the current research focuses on improving model performance, but our focus is different. 

        % The most visible advances in the application of AI to advance scientific research, often referred to as \emph{AI4Science}, particularly in the natural sciences, can be categorized at a high level into two parallel streams of research. On one hand, significant efforts are put into developing advanced models for specific applications, capable of, for example, predicting the protein structure from an amino acid sequence \cite{jumper2021alphafold}, generating novel molecules with desired properties \cite{loeffler2024reinvent}, or discovering new functional materials \cite{merchant2023scaling}. The other core stream focuses on assisting the researcher, for instance, by providing algorithm recommendations for sequential experimentation \cite{wang2023booverview}, using LLMs for assisting literature search and other mundane parts of the research process \cite{agarwal2025litllm}, or even assisting hypotheses generation \cite{wang2023scientific}. Our work relates to both streams, but does not deliver specific new tools. Instead, we focus on the research practice. We emphasize the development of frameworks that guide researchers in the effective use of these advances in the field, ensuring they serve as practical tools within the broader scientific process, and not just as isolated technological achievements. We assume that various computational models are available and continue to be developed, including domain-specific simulators, machine learning proxies, and AI-assisted experimentation and research tools. Our focus is on making these models accessible to practitioners across different scientific domains and ensuring they effectively support researchers. 

        % Unlike static production workflows, scientific inquiry is dynamic and requires constant adaptation, which necessitates a new approach to digital-physical frameworks. AI will necessarily play a central role here, providing assistance in optimising processes, analysing vast datasets, and even suggesting innovative avenues for exploration \cite{bobbili2023artificial, johnson2016malmo, buchanan1994role}.  With the advent of powerful LLMs and reasoning engines, a natural trend has been to consider them also in the context of research \cite{birhane2023science}, with an active focus on the analysis and production of natural language resources \cite{jiang2025applications} and other forms of purely digital information.         The challenge is to strike a balance between these digital tools and traditional physical measurements, optimising their combination for enhanced scientific exploration \cite{kluge2014combining}. In navigating this evolving landscape, researchers are exploring how to make the most of the diverse tools available, paving the way for a more agile and refined approach to their work.

        Scientific inquiry is dynamic and constantly evolving, unlike static production workflows. This demands new digital-physical frameworks that can adapt to changing questions and experimental conditions. AI is poised to play a central role in this shift. It helps optimize processes, analyze vast datasets, and suggest innovative directions for exploration \cite{bobbili2023artificial, johnson2016malmo, buchanan1994role}. With the advent of powerful LLMs and reasoning engines, AI is increasingly used not only for data analysis but also for producing and interpreting natural language resources \cite{jiang2025applications} and other forms of digital information \cite{birhane2023science}. AI could even be driving the discovery cycle itself \cite{swanson2024virtual,sparkes2010towards}. A key challenge is to combine these digital tools with traditional physical measurements in ways that enhance scientific exploration rather than fragment it \cite{kluge2014combining}.

        Within this evolving landscape, the field often referred to as \emph{AI4Science}, particularly in the natural sciences, there are two major streams of progress. The first focuses on developing advanced models for specific scientific applications, such as predicting protein structures from amino acid sequences \cite{jumper2021alphafold}, generating novel molecules with desired properties \cite{loeffler2024reinvent}, or predicting new functional materials \cite{merchant2023scaling}. The second stream aims at supporting researchers more directly, for example, through algorithm recommendations for sequential experimentation \cite{wang2023booverview}, the use of LLMs to assist in literature review and other routine tasks \cite{agarwal2025litllm}, or tools that aid hypothesis generation \cite{wang2023scientific,tiukova2024genesis}. Our work relates to both streams, but does not introduce new models or tools. Instead, we focus on the research practice itself: developing frameworks that help researchers effectively use these advances as integrated parts of the scientific process rather than isolated technological achievements. We assume that various computational models, including domain-specific simulators, machine learning proxies, and AI-assisted research tools, are available and continue to evolve. Our priority is to ensure these tools are accessible across scientific domains and genuinely useful to researchers.

        % In this context, VLs emerge as complex ecosystems that simulate physical experimentation, offering both significant opportunities and challenges.
        % In the landscape of scientific advancements, its labour-intensive nature often represents a prominent challenge. Recently, an avenue of transformation has risen through the digital realm, since a multitude of experiment components have become ripe for digitisation. Consider the scenario where a substantial portion of the experiment process undergoes digitisation, coupled with the computational power of Machine Learning (ML) techniques. This synergy results in a bridge between the digital and the physical, where computational tools, e.g. simulators, processors, or manipulators, operate analogously to their physical counterparts, defining this innovative ecosystem known as the "Virtual Laboratory" (VL). However, contrary to the assumption that digitisation and the integration of ML techniques inevitably alleviate this burden, numerous instances within the scientific community attest to the complexities inherent in this transformation \cite{singh2024analysis}. This divergence from anticipated outcomes underscores the profound disparity between traditional factory-style production pipelines and the dynamic, evolving nature of scientific inquiry. Unlike the former, where processes often remain static, scientific exploration thrives on adaptation and evolution, necessitating a paradigm shift in how we conceptualise and implement digital-physical frameworks. It is within this context that the concept of the VLs emerges - a complex ecosystem where computational tools simulate physical experimentation, presenting both tremendous opportunities and daunting challenges.

    % \subsection{Digitalisation of research}
