    Importantly, our framework complements the emerging trend of using LLMs as co-researchers or intelligent assistants. While recent work explores how LLMs can participate in the research process (e.g., guiding experiment design, generating hypotheses, or interpreting results), these systems currently lack structured integration into formalized experimental protocols. Our approach addresses this gap by focusing on the systematization of scientific workflows, providing a structured environment in which LLMs and other AI components could be reliably embedded, evaluated, and improved. This opens up the possibility of combining the free-form reasoning capabilities of LLMs with modular pipelines that enforce reproducibility and interpretability.

    Despite its strengths, our framework also highlights open challenges in building truly general-purpose AI-assisted research environments. Mapping arbitrary scientific tasks into a modular structure remains a work in progress, and many of the benefits of a unified software architecture would only be seen once the tools are broadly used. Our current prototypes demonstrate feasibility, but general usability across domains will require further development of shared standards, robust defaults, and interface conventions. The framework exposes many of the concrete difficulties that remain unsolved in the community, such as designing user-friendly tools for specifying workflows and ensuring semantic compatibility across modules.
