\section{Conclusion}
The time-domain formulation and adaptive algorithm presented in this work reframe active noise control as a problem of informed spatial trade-offs rather than uniform suppression. By embedding linear constraints directly into the optimization process via a Lagrangian approach, our method allows precise control over where in space noise reduction is prioritized, reflecting a shift from purely reactive to strategically targeted spatial filter design. The demonstrated gains at constrained locations without degrading overall performance affirm the value of this perspective. This framework allows for effective spatial control, particularly when coupled with future extensions such as kernel-based noise field interpolation, which promises to further reduce the burden of spatial sampling while enhancing control fidelity.

