\subsection{Dataset generation}\label{sec:dataset}


In this section, we describe the way in which we generate the training dataset. It consists of numerous input-output pairs, where each data pair is associated with a suitable distribution of the fluids A and B on a single stencil.

\begin{itemize}
\item
The input is a vector of size $m^3+1$. It contains the $m^3$ volume fractions associated with the given fluids distribution and the local Courant number in the positive $x$-direction.
The  local Courant number $\beta$ is sampled  in an interval $(C_{\rm min},C_{\rm max})\in [0,1]$.
\item
The output is the numerical flux in the   $x$-direction for the model equation (\ref{eq:prob}).
\end{itemize}
%Since normalization is a standard technique in VOF schemes and can greatly improve the neural network performances, we always assume, without any loss in generalization, normalized stencils, i.e. stencils centered in the origin %and where all the cells are cubes with volume 1.

Every input-output pair is obtained by purely geometrical considerations. %This strategy generates all the  data required by VOFML.
%It  is sufficient because the trained neural network, during a simulation, uses only very localized informations, where the interface between the fluids can be approximated by simple distributions as the ones in our dataset.
In most papers combining neural networks and VOF schemes
\cite{weymouth2010conservative,kawano2016simple,cahaly2024plic}, the training dataset is generated using regular shapes or functions. Here, instead, we are interested in training a neural network that can accurately predict the flux in the presence of both smooth and non-smooth interfaces, because in many applications both cases are present in the same simulation, in different regions of the domain.
% Examples of applications with such a property are related to fluid-structure interaction or simulations with more than two materials. We remark that we always refer to a problem with 2 fluids and assume that the
A generic case with more fluids can be solved by coupling 2-fluids sub-problems as discussed in \cite{ancellin2023extension}.
